**Lemma 22.5 (actual-law overlap).** For $t>0$ and $r\in(0,\delta]$, put

$$
I_\eta=I-\alpha_\eta,\qquad
E_\eta=\{u\in C([0,t]):u_a\in I_\eta\text{ for all }a\leq t\}.
\tag{22.10}
$$

Let $\Lambda_t$ be the common Lebesgue-start Brownian-height reference with
variance rate $\sigma^2$, and let $\Gamma_r(dv\mid u)$ be the common
conditional law of the lifted record $V$ from (22.6). Its pushforward to
wrapped records is the common phase kernel of L Theorem 64.1. Wrapping and
continuous lifting with the prescribed initial representative are Borel
inverses, so the same density applies in either representation. The joint
$(U,V)$ law under sign $\eta$ has density

$$
f_\eta(u)=e^{\lambda t}
 \phi(u_0+\alpha_\eta)\phi(u_t+\alpha_\eta)\mathbf1_{E_\eta}(u)
\tag{22.11}
$$

with respect to $\Lambda_t(du)\Gamma_r(dv\mid u)$, for the same positive
constant factor $e^{\lambda t}$. Thus the two laws are mutually absolutely
continuous on $E_+\cap E_-$, which has positive reference and positive mass
under both signs. Each sign also has a positive-probability singleton branch
where the other shifted height is inadmissible.

**Proof.** The stationary Doob-transform density from L Theorem 64.1,
translated by $\alpha_\eta$, is (22.11). The coefficient identity (22.6)
makes the conditional phase kernel identical, including all wrapped
windings. A compact interval in the nonempty intersection
$I_+\cap I_-$ has positive Brownian survival probability on every finite
horizon, and both endpoint factors in (22.11) are positive there. This
proves positive overlap. Its interval width is $w-2r\geq3w/4>0$.
Initial shifted points $U_0\in I_\eta\setminus I_{-\eta}$ give singleton
branches. Explicitly, under the plus law $K_0\in(\ell,\ell+2r)$
excludes the minus candidate, and under the minus law
$K_0\in(u-2r,u)$ excludes the plus candidate. Each interval has positive
$\pi$ mass. $\square$

For later use, the recovered candidate set at $a>0$ is precisely

$$
\mathcal C_a=\{\eta\in\{+1,-1\}:U_s+\alpha_\eta\in I
                     \text{ for every }0\leq s\leq a\}.
$$

For nonzero $r$ it is nonempty on actual good records, decreases with $a$,
and has either one or two elements. Membership is the strict lower and upper
bound on the minimum and maximum of the continuous candidate path on the
compact prefix, hence is a Borel prefix test. For $r=0$ the candidate
parameter is simply zero. These candidate tests use no supplied sign or
hidden height.


